6. What is @Profile? What if a required component is missing?

→ @Profile creates a bean only when the specified Spring profile is active.
→ Example: @Profile("dev") creates a development-specific implementation only in the dev profile.
→ If another required bean depends on it, application startup may fail with an unsatisfied dependency.
→ I would activate the correct profile, provide a default implementation, or use @ConditionalOnProperty or @ConditionalOnMissingBean where appropriate.

Example:

@Service
@Profile("dev")
public class DevNotificationService implements NotificationService {
}

→ If NotificationService is required in every environment, I would ensure that each environment has a suitable implementation.

