13. How do you implement global exception handling?

→ @RestControllerAdvice handles exceptions across controllers.
→ @ExceptionHandler defines how a particular exception should be handled.
→ Custom exceptions represent specific business errors.
→ I return meaningful HTTP status codes and consistent error responses without exposing internal details.

Example:

@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
class StudentNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    public StudentNotFoundException(Long id) {
        super("Student not found: " + id);
    }
}

@RestControllerAdvice
class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(StudentNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<String> handleNotFound(
            StudentNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                .body(ex.getMessage());
    }
}

→ If the student does not exist, I throw StudentNotFoundException.
→ The global handler returns HTTP 404 instead of an unhandled server error.
→ In production, I prefer a structured error response with an error code, message, and correlation ID.

