14. What is time complexity? Explain common search and sort algorithms.

→ Time complexity describes how an algorithm's execution time grows as input size increases.
→ Big O notation describes the growth rate as input size increases.

SEARCH ALGORITHMS

→ Linear Search: O(n) — checks elements one by one.
→ Binary Search: O(log n) — repeatedly halves a sorted search space.

SORTING ALGORITHMS

→ Bubble Sort: O(n²) average and worst case.
→ Selection Sort: O(n²) average and worst case.
→ Insertion Sort: O(n²) average and worst case; O(n) in the best case.
→ Merge Sort: O(n log n) in best, average, and worst cases.
→ Quick Sort: O(n log n) average; O(n²) worst case.
→ Heap Sort: O(n log n) in the worst case.

→ For large datasets, O(n log n) sorting algorithms are generally preferred.
→ Binary search requires sorted data or another structure that supports the necessary ordering.

